.TH CLOG_OPEN_FD_MP 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_open_fd_mp, clog_open_fd \- create a logger writing to an existing file descriptor
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog clog_open_fd_mp(int fd, clog_level_t min_level,
                  const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg,
                  ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs);
/* convenience wrapper; default allocator */
static inline clog clog_open_fd(int fd, clog_level_t min_level,
                  const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new logger that writes to an already-open file descriptor.
The logger does not own the file descriptor.  clog_close() does NOT
close it.  A logger that sits on an fd offers no log rotation.

Use fd=1 for stdout, and fd=2 for stderr.  Any other open writable fd
works too, and that includes a pipe and a connected socket.  See
clog_set_format(3) for the CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG case.  The peer end of fd
can go away later.  A write after that is dropped silently.  By
default it does not stop the process either: the logger writes to a
socket with MSG_NOSIGNAL, and it keeps SIGPIPE from a pipe away from
the process.  For a pipe, the first write from a thread other than
the async writer thread sets SIGPIPE to SIG_IGN for the process, when
its disposition is still SIG_DFL.  Opening the logger changes nothing,
and a logger on a regular file never touches SIGPIPE.  See
clog_set_sigpipe_policy(3) for the details, and for the policy that
leaves SIGPIPE entirely to the application.

You can open fd in blocking mode or in non-blocking mode.  A write on
a non-blocking fd can report EAGAIN or EWOULDBLOCK.  The calling
thread then waits for fd to become writable again, and it tries again.
It does not drop the rest of the record.  The library drops a record
only on a write error that it truly cannot recover from.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
fd         Open, writable file descriptor. Must be >= 0.
min_level  Messages below this level are silently dropped. Use
           CLOG_OFF to suppress all output except CLOG_FATAL, which
           is always written.
async_cfg  The configuration for async logging, or NULL for
           synchronous logging.  When it is not NULL, a log_*() call
           formats only its own message.  It gives the rest to a
           dedicated writer thread.  All loggers that clog_derive()
           makes from this logger also log asynchronously.  See
           clog_async_cfg_t below.  A struct of all zeros also turns
           async mode on.  This is different from a rotation config.
           Each zero field gets its own default.  Only a literal NULL
           keeps the logging synchronous.
             clog_async_cfg_t fields:
               queue_size        - the capacity of the message
                                    queue.  0 means an unbounded
                                    queue that never blocks a caller
                                    on a send.  A value above 0 means
                                    a bounded queue of that many
                                    messages.  A full bounded queue
                                    BLOCKS the caller until space
                                    frees up, and it never drops a
                                    message.
               flush_buffer_size - a count of bytes.  The writer
                                    thread flushes once its
                                    aggregation buffer reaches this
                                    size.  0 gives
                                    CLOG_DEFAULT_ASYNC_FLUSH_BUFFER_SIZE.
                                    The library has an internal cap
                                    of 16 MiB for each record.  A
                                    value above that cap still
                                    applies, and the growth ceiling
                                    of the aggregation buffer follows
                                    it.
               flush_interval_us - a uint64_t count of
                                    microseconds.  The writer thread
                                    flushes at least this often.  It
                                    does so even when the buffer has
                                    not reached flush_buffer_size.
                                    0 gives
                                    CLOG_DEFAULT_ASYNC_FLUSH_INTERVAL_US
                                    (200 ms).  Every value of the
                                    type is valid.  UINT64_MAX, or
                                    any interval too long for the
                                    clock to reach, turns the time
                                    trigger off: only
                                    flush_buffer_size, clog_flush()
                                    and clog_close() start a flush,
                                    and an idle writer thread sleeps
                                    until a record arrives.
mprocs     A custom allocator, which holds function pointers for
           malloc, free, calloc and realloc.  Pass NULL to use the
           default system allocator.  All four pointers must be set
           when mprocs is not NULL.  A struct that is only part
           filled makes the call return CLOG_INVALID, and it creates
           no logger.  The library copies the struct internally.  The
           caller can free it after this call.  All loggers that
           clog_derive() makes from this logger use the same
           allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new logger handle on success.
Returns CLOG_INVALID when fd is negative, closed, read-only or
otherwise not valid.  It also returns CLOG_INVALID when mprocs is not
valid, and when an allocation of memory fails.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The library does not duplicate the fd.  The caller must keep it open
for the whole life of the logger.
Every public function is fully thread-safe.  One lock serialises every
write to a given target.  The root logger and every logger derived
from it share that lock.
There are two supported ways to use fork(2) with clogger:
  - Call fork(2) before you open a logger.
  - Open a logger, call fork(2), and then call exec(3) immediately.
A process that continues with no exec(3) while a handle from before
the fork is live does not use a supported pattern.

The library also gives a defense in depth.  A handle that is open at
the time of such a fork is technically usable in the child.  Log,
derive and close operations on that handle work correctly on both
sides.  The async writer thread of a logger does not exist in a forked
child.  In the child, an inherited handle with async mode on changes
to synchronous logging.  This change is transparent.

This fallback is part of the fork protection of the library.  A build
with -DCCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0 does not include it.  Such a process
must not fork while it holds a live async logger.

When async_cfg is not NULL, CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG output is exempt from
batching.  That holds whatever queue_size, flush_buffer_size and
flush_interval_us say.  Every record and every backtrace frame still
keeps its own write() call.  Every format is exempt from batching on a
message-oriented socket: a SOCK_DGRAM, SOCK_SEQPACKET or SOCK_RDM
socket, such as a UDP socket or a Unix datagram socket.  The logger
checks the kind of fd when it opens.  Such a socket delivers each
write as one message, so every record goes out as one message of its
own, exactly as a synchronous logger sends it.  A record larger than
the socket can carry fails alone.  The library writes a "log record
truncated: 0 of <m> bytes written" record in its place, and the
records after it are still delivered.  A socket whose receiver has no
room makes the write wait for room, both where the system blocks the
send and where it refuses the send with ENOBUFS, as FreeBSD does for a
Unix datagram socket; the logger sends again until the message fits,
and a CLOG_FATAL call or the drain at exit waits only within its time
budget.  A CLOG_FATAL call always goes past the
queue.  It drains everything that earlier calls already queued.  It
then writes the fatal record itself synchronously, and only then stops
the process.

A process can end with exit(3), or a return from main(), while an async
logger is still open.  An exit handler that the library registers when
it loads then drains every async logger of the process as a CLOG_FATAL
call does: the queue first, then the batch, with a "log record
truncated: undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>): the
process stopped" record for what cannot be delivered.  The handler
runs after every exit handler that the application registers later,
so a record that such a handler logs is delivered too.  A budget of 5
seconds bounds the whole drain, so a descriptor that takes nothing
more, or threads that keep logging, delay the exit by that much at
most.  The budget bounds every lock that the drain takes too.  A
logger whose lock another thread holds past the budget is skipped,
and the records that it still holds can be lost.  Every write of the
drain is bounded as well.  A socket is written with MSG_DONTWAIT, and
a pipe or a FIFO is polled for room before each write of at most
PIPE_BUF bytes.  A terminal is written through a second, non-blocking
open of the same device, because room on a terminal does not mean room
for a whole write; the descriptor of the logger keeps its own mode.  A
descriptor in blocking mode whose reader stopped reading therefore
holds up the exit no longer than the budget allows.  The drain
takes the lock of a logger only when that logger has a queue or a
compression to drain: a thread that blocks for ever inside a write of
a synchronous logger delays no exit and no CLOG_FATAL call.
exit(3) that the writer thread of a logger reaches, from inside
a custom allocator, skips the drain of that one logger, because that
thread cannot serve it.  A record that an async logger receives after
its drain, from an exit handler that runs after the one of the library
or from a destructor, is written synchronously by the thread that logs
it, and clog_close(3) from there still works.  A logger whose drain
the budget cut off keeps its queue, and such a record can then be
lost.  _exit(2), a signal and abort(3) run no exit handler; call
clog_flush(3) or clog_close(3) first where the queued records
matter.

The kernel can accept only part of a write.  A peer that goes away in
the middle of a record does this, and so does a filesystem that has
just run out of space.  The bytes that it did take then go out with no
terminating newline for that record.  A synchronous logger writes that
newline itself.  The next record therefore starts a line of its own,
and nothing joins it onto the fragment.  The logger then writes a "log
record truncated: <n> of <m> bytes written" record that names the
loss.  An async logger keeps the bytes that the write did not take in
its batching buffer.  It offers them again on the next flush.  They
are the exact continuation of what did get out.  Once the condition
clears, the logger therefore completes the record and delivers every
record behind it.

A flush keeps the undelivered bytes only after an error that a later
attempt can outlast: ENOSPC, EDQUOT, EFBIG, ENOBUFS, ENOMEM, or EAGAIN
or EINTR that ends a wait.  Any other write error, such as EPIPE,
ECONNRESET or EBADF, fails the same way on every retry.  The flush
then drops the undelivered bytes and writes a "log record truncated:
undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>): write error <errno>"
record in their place, and the records after it go out as usual.  The
last flush of clog_close(3) has no later retry, so what it cannot
deliver is named the same way, with "the logger closed" as the reason.

A record can have to reach the descriptor before those bytes do.  The
library never writes such a record between the two halves of the
record that those bytes belong to.  There are two such cases.  One is
a CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG record, which the library never batches.  The other
is a record that takes the synchronous write path.  Each one first
gives the outstanding bytes one more attempt at delivery of its own.
When the descriptor still will not take them, it gives up on them, and
on every remaining line of the split record, backtrace lines included.
It then writes the missing newline of the split record, when a line is
open, and a "log record truncated: <n> undelivered bytes dropped"
record in their place.  A logfmt record whose backtrace lines are still
outstanding counts as split even when the bytes already written end
with the newline of its first line.  The
library keeps the records that the batch holds behind the split one.
A later flush delivers them, so they follow the record that it had to
write past them.
.fi
